  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Displacement due to conflict</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">On 09 December 2022 an Interagency Rapid Assessment needs was conducted to Conflict affected Displaced people in
Kodok Town, the assessment was led by OCHA. The team arrived Kodok at around 11:00 am and split into two teams.
One team conducted a humanitarian assessment for IDPs located new UNMISS COB while the other team went to Kodok
town. IDPs are mainly concentrated in two locations: Kodok town and beside the UNMISS COB. Others are scattered
under trees or have occupied government buildings. Many IDPs moved near UNMISS COB seeking protection. Several
meetings were conducted with the Commissioner, RRC Coordinator, Head of Security in Upper Nile, Minister of Youth
(also acting Minister of Gender), Minister of Economy, Police generals. The situation was calm, the team did not hear
any shelling activities in the area. The team observed barracks dedicated for SSPDF forces newly deployed in the area.
The team was told that SSPDF and Agwelek forces are located around Kodok to deter any attack. In the meeting with the
County Commissioner and RRC, both claimed the total number of conflict displaced population displaced to Kodok town
is between 40,000 to 42,000 people, and further reported that the total population of Fashoda County is about 95,000
people. They also reported that the White Army elements have been pushed back from Fashoda areas of Oriny, Dubo,
Aywang-gen to Owachi in Payikan County.
There was an active movement between IDPS settlement near the COB and Kodok town. People pay 50 SSP one way to
reach the town using canoes. People rely heavily on the services provided in the town, including latrines and market.
Civilians have freedom of movement within the town and to areas far including Melut and Renk. Transportation fees to</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="2">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">To provide life-saving  Health and WASH interventions to the displaced persons in Kodok.</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="3">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">We will target the most vulnerable, displaced families who are displaced by the conflict and are sheltering IDP sites or outside, mainly under trees within host communities, or in open buildings. Specifically this will include displaced households with elderly, PLWs and children under 5, and people with specific needs.</narrative>
  </description>
